2. User segments, problems, jobs to be done

Primary: independent professional

• Freelancers and consultants handling several clients at once.
• Recruiters and estate agents handling many follow-ups.
• Agency owners and sales professionals who promise proposals, estimates, documents, and callbacks.
• Jobs to be done: “When I tell someone I’ll do something, I want to capture it without changing apps, and be reminded before I disappoint them.”

Secondary: personal organizer

• Parents, household organizers, volunteers and busy individuals who frequently promise personal tasks.
• Jobs to be done: “Help me remember what I owe people without turning my personal life into project management.”

Pain points

1. Capturing promises during a conversation is cumbersome.
2. A conventional to-do title omits who is waiting and why.
3. Deadlines are implicit: “before the meeting,” “this week,” “next time we talk.”
4. Reminders fire once and disappear even though the obligation remains open.
5. Users forget to follow up when a promised task depends on someone else.
6. People cannot easily answer: “What do I still owe Sarah?”

Differentiation hypothesis

People will pay for promise-specific follow-through, not for a generic to-do app with a microphone button. Validate through user interviews and observed weekly retention before expanding scope.

