5. User journeys

Journey A: instant voice capture

1. User opens app and taps prominent microphone control (or invokes an app shortcut).
2. User says: “I promised Anna I’d send the finished website by Friday at four.”
3. Audio is transcribed with appropriate speech permissions; editable transcript appears.
4. Local model extracts title, person, time phrase, and details into a typed schema.
5. Deterministic date resolver interprets “Friday at four” relative to capture time and locale. If ambiguous AM/PM or which Friday, ask for confirmation.
6. Preview sheet shows: Send finished website · Anna · Fri 16:00 with quick edits.
7. User taps Save. Record persists, notifications schedule, success haptic and optional spoken confirmation.
8. Home screen now displays the promise in Upcoming.

Acceptance: On supported hardware/locale, successful utterance produces a preview; user can fix mistakes before saving; no date guessed without transparent warning.

Journey B: multiple promises in a single statement

User says: “I told Alex I’d send the pricing tomorrow and I owe Jamie a call sometime next week.”

• AI proposes two distinct draft items.
• One has a precise due day; the other requires a chosen date or may remain unscheduled.
• User may save one, both, or neither. Never join them accidentally.

Journey C: dependency / waiting on someone

“I’ll send Tom the signed contract once Legal approves it.”

• Commitment: send signed contract to Tom.
• nextAction: wait for Legal approval, optionally represented as waiting-on text/person.
• Status: waiting, only after user approval.
• Reminders are configurable waiting check-ins, not an invented contract deadline.

Journey D: checking with Siri

User: “Siri, what do I owe Anna in Promise Tracker?”

• A supported App Intent looks up local promises filtered by a resolved app person entity.
• Siri may speak a short summary or open a relevant view, subject to the OS’s supported runtime behavior.
• If no person matches, present a disambiguation or safe result, not an accidental disclosure.

Journey E: closing a promise

User taps Done on a notification or promise detail; app marks completed, cancels pending reminders, records event, allows undo/reopen and shows completion summary. Siri completion must resolve the specific item unambiguously.

Journey F: catching up

At an opt-in briefing time, user receives: “You have 2 promises due today, 1 overdue, and 2 waiting for responses.” The daily view lists them and suggests a next action. No LLM invocation is required just to count items.

Journey G: no Apple Intelligence available

User types/dictates into the native text entry area. Basic deterministic rules identify obvious dates and names only when reliable; otherwise a simple manual form requests title, person and date. The user can still save/search/remind. No forced cloud fallback.

