13. Privacy, security, compliance

• Describe precisely what Apple on-device inference does and whether any selected model profile can process remotely. The marketed “fully local” claim must match actual runtime routing.
• Core records stored locally with iOS Data Protection and appropriate keychain storage for secrets (where any exist).
• No app-controlled server account or backend required for MVP.
• Explicit permission gates for microphone, speech recognition, notifications, Contacts and Reminders when needed.
• No automatic scraping of Messages, WhatsApp, calls or email.
• No background always-on recording or undocumented call access.
• Optional future integrations undergo independent permission, platform-policy and security review.
• Store subscription entitlement state using Apple-supported transaction APIs, not a homemade trust flag.
• Data export, in-app erasure and transparency about device backups/sync.
• Configure app privacy labels according to actual SDK/network behavior; avoid third-party analytics that collect raw sensitive content.
• Add privacy policy, terms, support contact and App Store subscription disclosures before release.
• Review GDPR obligations when targeting EU customers, especially if any optional telemetry, hosted service, or sync creates a controllership/processor relationship.
• Secure deep links and intent parameters; check entity access before presenting user data in lock-screen contexts.

