17. Final acceptance criteria (release-blocking)

1. Users can create/edit/delete/complete a commitment entirely offline.
2. Spoken and typed text can create reviewable structured drafts on supported devices.
3. When AI is unavailable, manual entry is fully functional and explained.
4. User can view all open promises linked to a person.
5. Unspecific dates are not silently turned into invented exact deadlines.
6. A stored promise can generate a local reminder that is cancelled/replaced when promise state changes.
7. Siri/App Intents can perform a documented subset of actions on a real supported iPhone; unsupported utterances fail safely.
8. Double execution of a Done intent does not corrupt the item or create double activity events.
9. No messaging, email or calendar actions occur without explicit user instruction and necessary permission.
10. Existing user-owned records remain accessible/exportable if the subscription ends.
11. Source text and contact names are not sent to a developer server or logged in third-party analytics by default.
12. Standard, accessibility, denied-permission and no-model journeys are tested.
13. App survives termination/relaunch, schema migration and common failed-save scenarios without data loss.
14. Subscription purchase, restore and expiration have verified StoreKit behavior.
15. No real-world claim such as “fully private,” “100% accurate” or “works with any Siri phrase” is made without technical evidence.

